      "0.80-0.94 (ambiguous but likely)": 32,
      "below 0.70 (guessing)": 0,
      "unassigned gap 0.70-0.79": 0
    },
    "statement": "Across the 379 elements of the recorded run the score takes 7 distinct values in [0.85, 0.97]. It never enters the prompt's bottom band -- 0 elements score below 0.70, the value the rubric reserves for a guess -- and it uses the middle 0.80-0.94 band for only 32 of them. Of those 379 elements, 298 are in scope for a golden and every one has been human-audited; exactly 1 is invented, and it also holds the lowest score. That separation survives 14 same-configuration repeats (966 elements, 1 distinct element below 0.9), so it is not an artifact of one draw -- but it is still an observation rather than a validated threshold, because one positive case cannot establish a false-negative rate. That is why the system gates on explicit human verification instead of on this score.",
    "what_the_score_does_not_register": "The audit's 39 real_split_title rows are real content whose titles were broken by multi-column interleaving, the layout condition that most degrades transcription in this corpus. Every one of them scores at or above 0.95. Whatever the low end of the score tracks, it is not layout difficulty."
  }
